# Sprint 13 — SOC Architecture Expansion & Baseline

## Objective

Establish a known-good baseline of the existing Enterprise SOC environment and prepare the architecture for expansion into a multi-layer Security Operations Center lab.

## Existing Infrastructure

| Host | Role | Operating System |
|---|---|---|
| DC01 | Domain Controller / DNS | Windows Server 2022 |
| WIN11-01 | Enterprise Workstation | Windows 11 Enterprise |
| SIEM01 | Splunk SIEM | Windows Server 2022 |

## Baseline Validation

The following components were validated before expansion:

- Active Directory
- DNS
- Group Policy
- Sysmon
- Windows Security logging
- PowerShell logging
- Splunk Universal Forwarder
- Splunk Enterprise ingestion
- SIEM searches and telemetry visibility

## Architecture Expansion

The following systems are planned:

- WIN11-02 — Additional enterprise endpoint
- KALI01 — Controlled adversary simulation platform
- FW01 — pfSense firewall
- IDS01 — Network intrusion detection
- LINUX01 — Future Linux enterprise workload

## Network Segmentation

Future network segmentation was designed using:

- CORP-LAN
- ATTACK-NET
- Firewall-controlled routing

Existing production lab networking remained unchanged during this sprint.

## Security Operations Goal

The expanded environment is designed to support an end-to-end SOC workflow:

Attack → Telemetry → Detection → Alert → Triage → Investigation → Containment → Remediation → Documentation

## Validation

The existing environment remained operational following Sprint 13 preparation.

## Outcome

Sprint 13 established a stable baseline and architecture plan for expanding the existing detection-engineering environment into a complete SOC operations lab.
